=== REASONING ===
Why this prompt is log-only and why the test contract drives Prompt 02:

  1. Prompt 00 approved bug-fix Change 1: stop dropping stale and legacy
     values from GetAll in a way that silently zeroes the dashboard.
  2. Tests that codify the new contract MUST be authored before the
     implementation so Prompt 02's red->green transition proves the bug
     is fixed and stays fixed under regression.
  3. Per the prompt body: "Do not commit failing tests in this prompt."
     Therefore this prompt only SPECIFIES the matrix; Prompt 02 will add
     the tests AND change live_store.go in the same commit so the suite
     stays green at every commit boundary.

Contract that Prompt 02 must implement (this is the spec, not the code):

  R1. GetAll(LiveSignalReadDistributed) returns a UNION of L1 and L2 values
      keyed by signal name. Neither layer's entries are silently dropped
      based on freshness. L1-only signals appear in the result. L2-only
      signals appear in the result. Per-signal merge applies to overlap.

  R2. Legacy scalar L2 values (Timestamp.IsZero()) MUST be returned by
      GetAll/GetSignal in distributed mode. They retain their zero
      timestamp so callers and IsLiveSignalFresh can see they are unknown
      freshness, but they are never silently omitted from the response.
      This satisfies ADR-007 "Keep existing Redis scalar HSET values
      readable indefinitely; they are replaced naturally on the next
      telemetry write" and "timestamp-less legacy Redis values [have]
      unknown freshness, not fresh data" (informational, not a drop).

  R3. Stale L2 values (Timestamp older than LiveSignalFreshnessThreshold)
      MUST be returned by GetAll/GetSignal in distributed mode. Their
      timestamp is preserved so IsLiveSignalFresh reports them as not
      fresh; callers decide what to do. This is a behavior change from
      the current freshSignalValues filter and is the core of bug-fix
      Change 1 from phase-38-00.

  R4. Per-signal merge rule when the same signal name exists in both L1
      and L2: the value with the NEWER non-zero Timestamp wins. Ties on
      identical non-zero Timestamps prefer the L2 value (cross-pod
      authoritative cache) and this tie-break must be asserted explicitly
      in one test so it cannot drift later. A zero-timestamp legacy
      value loses to ANY value with a non-zero Timestamp regardless of
      which layer it came from (legacy is unknown-freshness, not zero
      time). When neither side has a Timestamp, the L1 value wins
      (L1 is the local hot path and is the more recent observation in
      practice when L2 has only legacy scalars).

  R5. GetSignal(LiveSignalReadDistributed) MUST follow R1-R4 for the
      single requested signal name (i.e. it must consult both L1 and L2
      and apply the merge rule, not "L2-or-fall-back-to-L1").

  R6. GetAll(LiveSignalReadLocal) and GetSignal(LiveSignalReadLocal) MUST
      remain L1-only and MUST NOT call into the L2 client at all
      (LiveSignalReadLocal behavior unchanged; the L1-only path is
      unchanged; the local read path never reads Redis).
      Verified by injecting a Redis client that fails on any call and
      asserting LiveSignalReadLocal still succeeds.

  R7. Redis read errors in distributed mode MUST still surface to the
      caller as wrapped errors (no silent swallow). The merge-with-L1
      fallback applies only to the empty-result case, not to the
      error case.

  R8. IsLiveSignalFresh is unchanged. Stale, zero-timestamp, and nil
      values remain "not fresh". This prompt does not modify or replace
      that helper.

ADR alignment:

  - ADR-001 unaffected. signal_log remains durable history; tests do not
    introduce snapshot-table reads.
  - ADR-007 preserved and tightened. Legacy/stale L2 values become
    informational (timestamp surfaced) rather than silently dropped.
    LIVE_SIGNAL_STORE_MODE=local rollback path remains L1-only and is
    explicitly tested.

